INT. CATHEDRAL BELL TOWER - DAY

Stone steps spiral up into a chamber of oak beams and ancient dust. Light falls through louvered slits in thin gold bars. In the center, a bronze bell the size of a truck, tongue wrapped in canvas.

SERGEANT IRENE VASCONCELOS (30s, face smeared with soot, helmet netted with dry grass) lies prone at a slit window, rifle braced, a cold cup of tea beside her elbow. Below, a ruined square lies empty under the sun.

PRIVATE HAMZA QURESHI (19, spotter, binoculars on a strap) kneels at the next slit, lips moving silently as he counts windows across the plaza.

CORPORAL OTTOLINE DE WILDE (40s, radio operator, a darned sock peeking from her boot) sits against the bell with the handset in her lap. She is knitting something small and gray.

Nobody speaks for a long time.

Down in the square, a dog crosses, trots, stops, sniffs a boot. Hamza lowers his binoculars. Irene does not move.

OTTOLINE
Was a good dog, once.

IRENE
Mm.

The dog moves on. Hamza lifts the glasses. Ottoline's needles click. The bell ticks softly as the stone cools.

HAMZA
(barely audible)
Four minutes.

Irene breathes out, slowly, and in. She touches the cold tea with one finger, not drinking it.
